**Definition 4.2 (common processing after the last source-bearing input).**
Acquisition and the algebra are fixed before the next public request is
supplied. For each public request $q$, subsequent outcome-retaining
processing is one common CPTP map $\Phi_q$ on $\widehat{\mathcal B}$,
with absorbing input $\bot$ remaining stopped. Independent ancillary
states, computation, fresh randomness and internal feedback on actual
records can be absorbed into it. Arbitrary source-independent exact-time
output capability is granted; the output algebra need not be finite.
Every acquisition stop, erroneous rejection and executed failure remains
an output sector, with its declared successor. There is no renormalization
by acceptance. Discarding fields only defines a necessary reduced test.

No later source-dependent channel, actuator configuration, original-height
query, waveform callback, source-sensitive timer, programme setting,
producer port or readable share arrives outside $\mathcal B$. This is
essential. The ideal source-indexed family $\mathcal I_x$ does not itself
constitute one common channel on an isolated finite handoff. Supplying its
source-bound execution as an additional future service changes this access
contract. In particular the extra producer phase port of [RA][RA] §§13–17
is outside it.

